// `let (a, b) = f()` used to compile and bind nothing: the let handler reads a
// single identifier for the name, so the `(` was taken as the name and the
// elements never existed. The statement produced no diagnostic, and you only
// found out at the first use of one of the names.
//
// There is nothing to destructure -- the language has no tuple type and OP_RET
// carries one operand -- so a function returning several values returns a
// struct and the caller reads its fields.
fn f() -> i64 { ret 7 }

fn main() {
    let (a, b) = f()
    print("{a}\n")
}
